1. Descriptive Statistics (The "What Happened?" phase)
   Before you can predict the future, you have to describe the present.
   Measures of Central Tendency: Mean: The average (sensitive to outliers).
                                 Median: The middle value (great for skewed data like salaries).
                                 Mode: The most frequent value (useful for categorical data).
 
   Measures of Dispersion (Spread): Range: The gap between the max and min.
                                    Variance: How far each point is from the mean.
                                    Standard Deviation: The average distance of data points from the mean. 
                                                        A low standard deviation means data is clustered 
                                                        A high standard deviation means it's spread out.

   Interquartile Range (IQR): The middle 50% of the data (used to identify outliers).

2. Probability Distributions (Data usually follows a pattern)
   Recognizing the pattern helps you choose the right analysis tool.
   Normal Distribution (The Bell Curve): Most natural phenomena follow this. 
          Key rule: the 68-95-99.7 Rule.68% of data falls within 1 standard deviation.95% falls within 2 standard deviations.
   Skewness: Is your data "leaning"?Right-skewed: Long tail to the right (e.g., household income).
   Left-skewed: Long tail to the left (e.g., age of retirement).
   Binomial Distribution: For "Yes/No" or "Success/Failure" outcomes (e.g., Did the customer click the ad?).
3. Inferential Statistics (The "What does it mean?" phase)
   This is where you take a sample and make a conclusion about a whole population.
   Hypothesis Testing:
       Null Hypothesis (H_0): The "status quo." (e.g., "This new website design has NO effect on sales.")
       Alternative Hypothesis (H_a): What you are trying to prove. (e.g., "The new design increases sales.")
       P-Value: The probability that your results happened by random chance.
       Rule of thumb: If p < 0.05, your result is "statistically significant" (you can reject the Null).
       Confidence Intervals: Instead of saying "Sales will be $100," you say "We are 95% confident sales will be between $95 and $105.
4. Relationship ModelingCorrelation: Measures the strength and direction of a relationship between two variables (ranges from -1 to 1).
   Note: Correlation does not equal causation
   Linear Regression: Predicting a dependent variable (y) based on an independent variable (x)
   R-Squared: Tells you how much of the variation in y is explained by your model.

