The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) is emphasized: it states that the distribution of a large-sample mean will approximate a normal distribution, even if the original data are not normal. 
This “fundamental theorem” justifies using normal-based techniques for inference on many problems.

For inference, we cover hypothesis testing: null/alternative hypotheses, p-values, and confidence intervals. 
A p-value is the probability of observing data as extreme as we did under the null hypothesis.
Smaller p-values indicate stronger evidence against the null. (Note: p-values do NOT measure the probability the hypothesis is true.) 
We discuss Type I/II errors and choosing significance levels. For example, testing whether a new marketing campaign changed conversion rates involves a null hypothesis of no effect, computing a p-value, and deciding to reject or not.

We also cover correlation and regression.
The Pearson correlation coefficient quantifies linear association between two variables (ranges –1 to +1). 
We caution that “correlation does not imply causation” – two variables may move together without one causing the other. 
We introduce simple linear regression (fitting y = mx + b) as a predictive tool, and mention extensions to multivariate regression.

Advanced topics include analysis of variance (ANOVA) for comparing means across groups, and basics of nonparametric tests (e.g. Chi-square, Mann-Whitney). 
We examine sampling techniques (random sampling, stratified sampling) and when each is appropriate.

Examples: We use scenarios like A/B tests (comparing two website designs using t-tests), and the “Beer and Diapers” classic example to show spurious correlation. 
Datasets like normal_dist.csv illustrate CLT by simulating sample means.
